[?2004l[?2004h> s += '''
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Escalating a ticket
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ```
[?2004l[?2004h> python -m deskline.cli escalate --tenant northwind --ticket 1042 --reason "SLA breached, customer chasing"
[?2004l[?2004h> ```
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The command reports the new status, tenant-hook delivery ID, and separate hook/on-call delivery
[?2004l[?2004h> outcomes. It exits non-zero for unknown tenants/tickets, resolved/closed tickets, or either delivery
[?2004l[?2004h> failure. Delivery failures do **not** undo the escalation or its internal note; the relay error is
[?2004l[?2004h> printed to stderr and both destinations are attempted independently. Avoid blindly retrying: a
[?2004l[?2004h> retry adds another escalation note and may duplicate a delivery that already succeeded.
[?2004l[?2004h> '''
[?2004l[?2004h> p.write_text(s)
[?2004l[?2004h> p = Path('docs/escalation_notifications.md')
[?2004l[?2004h> s = p.read_text()
[?2004l[?2004h> s += '''
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Context boundaries and delivery outcomes
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Context is captured before changing the ticket or writing the escalation note. The queue therefore
[?2004l[?2004h> shows tickets already waiting, rather than adding this new escalation to its own context.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The example queue above illustrates the **deskline on-call** event. That internal destination gets
[?2004l[?2004h> the desk-wide related-ticket panel and escalation queue. Tenant hooks must not disclose other
[?2004l[?2004h> tenants' data (see `tenancy.md`): their `related` and `queue` contain only tickets belonging to the
[?2004l[?2004h> recipient tenant. Tenant filtering happens **before** the usual five-row limits, so foreign tickets
[?2004l[?2004h> do not crowd out the recipient's own context. The desk-wide CLI panel and hourly digest are unchanged.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> `escalate()` returns separate `delivered` (hook) and `oncall_delivered` flags. `delivery_id` is the
[?2004l[?2004h> hook relay response's ID, or null if the hook failed. `hook_error` and `oncall_error` carry relay
